=============================
COMPLETE HASHMAP vs HASHTABLE THEORY
=============================

1. HASHING
Hashing is a technique used to store data in key-value pairs.
A hash function converts a key into an integer index.
This index decides where the value will be stored in the array.

Goal:
Fast insertion, deletion and search (O(1) average).

--------------------------------------------------

2. HASHMAP (java.util.HashMap)

Definition:
HashMap is a class that implements Map interface and stores key-value pairs.

Features:
- Allows one null key
- Allows multiple null values
- Not synchronized (Not thread-safe)
- Does not maintain order
- Faster than Hashtable
- Default capacity = 16
- Default load factor = 0.75

--------------------------------------------------

3. INTERNAL WORKING OF HASHMAP

Step 1: hashCode() is called on key.
Step 2: Hash value is processed (hash spreading).
Step 3: Index calculated using:
        index = (n - 1) & hash
        (n must be power of 2)

Step 4: If bucket empty → insert.
Step 5: If collision:
        - Compare keys using equals()
        - If same key → replace value
        - Else → add to LinkedList

Java 8 Improvement:
If bucket size >= 8 AND capacity >= 64
→ LinkedList converts to Red-Black Tree.
This improves worst-case time from O(n) to O(log n).

--------------------------------------------------

4. TIME COMPLEXITY

Operation     Average      Worst (Java 8+)
put()         O(1)         O(log n)
get()         O(1)         O(log n)
remove()      O(1)         O(log n)

Before Java 8 worst case = O(n)

--------------------------------------------------

5. LOAD FACTOR

Load Factor = size / capacity

Default = 0.75

Threshold = capacity × load factor
Example:
16 × 0.75 = 12

If entries > 12 → Rehashing occurs.

--------------------------------------------------

6. REHASHING

When threshold crossed:
- Capacity doubles (16 → 32 → 64)
- New array created
- All elements reinserted

Time complexity during rehash = O(n)

--------------------------------------------------

7. HASHCODE & EQUALS CONTRACT

Rule 1:
If two objects are equal (equals() returns true)
→ Their hashCode() must be same.

Rule 2:
If hashCode same → objects may or may not be equal.

If equals overridden but hashCode not overridden
→ HashMap may behave incorrectly.

--------------------------------------------------

8. FAIL-FAST BEHAVIOR

HashMap iterator is fail-fast.
If structure modified during iteration
→ ConcurrentModificationException thrown.

--------------------------------------------------

9. HASHTABLE (java.util.Hashtable)

Definition:
Legacy class introduced in Java 1.0.

Features:
- No null key allowed
- No null value allowed
- Synchronized (Thread-safe)
- Slower than HashMap
- Legacy (rarely used today)

--------------------------------------------------

10. HASHMAP vs HASHTABLE

Feature            HashMap         Hashtable
--------------------------------------------------
Thread Safe        No              Yes
Null Key           Allowed (1)     Not Allowed
Null Value         Allowed         Not Allowed
Performance        Faster          Slower
Introduced         Java 1.2        Java 1.0
Iterator           Fail-Fast       Enumerator

--------------------------------------------------

11. WHY CAPACITY IS POWER OF 2?

Because index calculation:
(n - 1) & hash

Bitwise AND works efficiently when n is power of 2.
It distributes keys uniformly.

--------------------------------------------------

12. MODERN ALTERNATIVE

Instead of Hashtable use:
ConcurrentHashMap

It provides thread safety with better performance.

--------------------------------------------------

